# Python
# NOTE: .gitignore has no inline comments -- everything after the pattern is
# part of the pattern. `.venv/   # self-ignores...` therefore matched a
# directory literally named that, i.e. nothing, and .venv/ was ignored only by
# the .gitignore the venv module writes inside itself. Comments go on their
# own line.
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# Raw datasets: large, and reproducible via data/download_*.sh
data/raw/

# Processed data is regenerated by train/ scripts and stays out of git, except
# the three small files below. The Dockerfile does not copy train/, so nothing
# inside the image can rebuild an artifact -- without these committed, a clone
# plus `docker compose up` starts with 4 of 7 agents and needs a host Python
# toolchain to get further, which is exactly what the Docker path exists to
# avoid. Together they cost 842 KB.
#
#   battery_features.csv          the battery agent and telemetry simulator
#   supply_chain_shipments.csv    the risk agent, and compound risk through it
#   fleet_readiness.csv           the Electrification Readiness page
data/processed/*
!data/processed/battery_features.csv
!data/processed/supply_chain_shipments.csv
!data/processed/fleet_readiness.csv

# Trained model artifacts. The two vision models are 98 MB each and stay out --
# they are the one thing a clone cannot have, so `/ready` reports 503 with
# `quality` degraded until you train them, which is the honest state.
#
# The two small ones are committed: battery_rul_model.pkl (221 KB) and
# risk_model.pkl (2.2 MB). Without them CI executed no model code at all while
# still spending minutes installing tensorflow-cpu, and a Docker-only user had
# no way to produce them, since train/ is not in the image.
backend/models/*.pkl
backend/models/*.h5
backend/models/*.keras
!backend/models/battery_rul_model.pkl
!backend/models/risk_model.pkl

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.vscode/
*.log

# Env
.env
